WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.017 --> 00:00:02.600 line:15%
(gentle music)

2
00:00:09.480 --> 00:00:11.220
We are in The Harvard Plate Stacks.

3
00:00:11.220 --> 00:00:15.300
So this is the top floor of three floors

4
00:00:15.300 --> 00:00:18.930
that holds over 600,000 glass plate negatives

5
00:00:18.930 --> 00:00:20.220
of the night sky.

6
00:00:20.220 --> 00:00:23.460 line:15%
The collection did not start to what we know today

7
00:00:23.460 --> 00:00:28.020 line:15%
until the bequest given to us by Anna Palmer Draper.

8
00:00:28.020 --> 00:00:32.700
She and Henry Draper, her husband, had their own observatory

9
00:00:32.700 --> 00:00:34.860
in Hastings-on-Hudson,

10
00:00:34.860 --> 00:00:38.370
and they were both interested in spectra.

11
00:00:38.370 --> 00:00:42.060
So if you take the light from a star,

12
00:00:42.060 --> 00:00:46.260
you have it go through a prism and hit a glass plate,

13
00:00:46.260 --> 00:00:48.480
that'll capture a spectra.

14
00:00:48.480 --> 00:00:51.600
It'll look black and white in our collection,

15
00:00:51.600 --> 00:00:54.180
but it's in a rainbow color.

16
00:00:54.180 --> 00:00:56.130
And they were trying to figure out

17
00:00:56.130 --> 00:00:58.080
what the stars were made out of.

18
00:00:58.080 --> 00:00:59.760
We knew here on Earth

19
00:00:59.760 --> 00:01:02.760
that each element has its own unique signature.

20
00:01:02.760 --> 00:01:06.720
So we can use that by comparing spectra

21
00:01:06.720 --> 00:01:11.720
of known elements here on earth with spectras of the stars

22
00:01:12.030 --> 00:01:16.650
to figure out what unique elements are inside of each star.

23
00:01:16.650 --> 00:01:18.990
So there was a conference in New York City,

24
00:01:18.990 --> 00:01:22.080
specifically gathering the greatest minds

25
00:01:22.080 --> 00:01:24.360
of astronomy across America.

26
00:01:24.360 --> 00:01:28.860
And our director at the time, Edward Pickering, went.

27
00:01:28.860 --> 00:01:32.520
And unfortunately, Mr. Draper actually caught ill,

28
00:01:32.520 --> 00:01:35.490
and five days later, after this big gathering,

29
00:01:35.490 --> 00:01:37.230
he passed away.

30
00:01:37.230 --> 00:01:42.230
And Anna, she wanted to continue their scientific legacy

31
00:01:42.540 --> 00:01:44.370
after his death,

32
00:01:44.370 --> 00:01:46.740
but realized that she couldn't devote

33
00:01:46.740 --> 00:01:48.780
the same amount of time that she used to

34
00:01:48.780 --> 00:01:50.790
in order to actually get the work

35
00:01:50.790 --> 00:01:53.880
that she and Henry were hoping to get done.

36
00:01:53.880 --> 00:01:58.880 line:15%
But luckily, Edward Pickering was able to convince her

37
00:01:59.100 --> 00:02:02.460 line:15%
that Harvard could continue on with this legacy.

38
00:02:02.460 --> 00:02:07.460
That then created "The Henry Draper Memorial" catalog,

39
00:02:08.040 --> 00:02:12.840
and one of the central parts of our collection today,

40
00:02:12.840 --> 00:02:17.220
where 1/3 of the 600,000 glass plate negatives

41
00:02:17.220 --> 00:02:20.190
are actually these particular plates

42
00:02:20.190 --> 00:02:22.920
that are taking different stars

43
00:02:22.920 --> 00:02:27.920
and letting us see what they are made out of

44
00:02:28.830 --> 00:02:32.670
by taking their light and casting it into a spectra.

45
00:02:32.670 --> 00:02:35.370
One of these women who would become central

46
00:02:35.370 --> 00:02:39.420
in "The Henry Draper Catalogue" was Annie Jump Cannon.

47
00:02:39.420 --> 00:02:43.290
Annie was lucky enough to go to Wellesley,

48
00:02:43.290 --> 00:02:47.040
one of the earliest women's schools in America,

49
00:02:47.040 --> 00:02:51.420
and study at a brand-new observatory there.

50
00:02:51.420 --> 00:02:55.110
In 1896, she actually started volunteering here

51
00:02:55.110 --> 00:02:57.270
at the Harvard College Observatory.

52
00:02:57.270 --> 00:03:00.960
Annie was tasked with going through not hundreds,

53
00:03:00.960 --> 00:03:04.590
but hundreds of thousands of stars

54
00:03:04.590 --> 00:03:07.410
and to put them into stellar classifications.

55
00:03:07.410 --> 00:03:10.110
So she's looking at the spectra plates,

56
00:03:10.110 --> 00:03:12.000
and she saw this pattern,

57
00:03:12.000 --> 00:03:14.490
and that ended up giving us

58
00:03:14.490 --> 00:03:17.010
the Harvard stellar standard sequence,

59
00:03:17.010 --> 00:03:21.323
or the Harvard sequence, OBAFGKM, that we still use today

60
00:03:24.210 --> 00:03:27.510
to let us know from a star spectrum,

61
00:03:27.510 --> 00:03:32.510
what kind of star is it, what elements is it made out of,

62
00:03:32.580 --> 00:03:37.580
and how along its evolution will it go.

63
00:03:38.040 --> 00:03:40.680
So on one plate, she has hundreds,

64
00:03:40.680 --> 00:03:43.110
if not thousands, of stars,

65
00:03:43.110 --> 00:03:47.040
each with its own spectra cast onto the glass.

66
00:03:47.040 --> 00:03:49.890
And inside of that spectra with magnification,

67
00:03:49.890 --> 00:03:52.830
Annie could see the absorption and emission lines

68
00:03:52.830 --> 00:03:55.890
that would let her, just by sight,

69
00:03:55.890 --> 00:03:58.440
be able to realize which one

70
00:03:58.440 --> 00:04:01.740
of the stellar classifications she would assign to them.

71
00:04:01.740 --> 00:04:03.660
Over her lifetime, she would actually classify

72
00:04:03.660 --> 00:04:05.970
over 200,000 stars.

73
00:04:05.970 --> 00:04:10.920
She had this amazing knack that never was surpassed

74
00:04:10.920 --> 00:04:14.460
in our history and was able to just,

75
00:04:14.460 --> 00:04:17.580
with that little blip of spectra,

76
00:04:17.580 --> 00:04:21.547
be able to know which category these stars fell into.

77
00:04:21.547 --> 00:04:25.080
"The Henry Draper Catalogue," which is still used today,

78
00:04:25.080 --> 00:04:26.970
is pivotal in science.

79
00:04:26.970 --> 00:04:29.880
Annie would actually publish nine volumes

80
00:04:29.880 --> 00:04:34.800
that would be these tables of a HD number,

81
00:04:34.800 --> 00:04:38.520
Henry Draper number, that would tell you what star it is,

82
00:04:38.520 --> 00:04:41.820
and then it would give you a classification letter

83
00:04:41.820 --> 00:04:42.870
for each one of these.

84
00:04:42.870 --> 00:04:47.280
And now with these classifications, not only were we able

85
00:04:47.280 --> 00:04:51.000
to start parsing out what stars are made out of,

86
00:04:51.000 --> 00:04:55.203
but also, the ways that our universe is evolving.

87
00:04:56.282 --> 00:04:58.865
(gentle music)

88
00:05:00.182 --> 00:05:03.599
(gentle music continues)

